---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const encrypted = [
  "Your CV",
  "Evaluation reports",
  "Cover letter drafts",
  "Interview notes",
  "Saved contacts",
  "Job description text you paste in",
  "Your own AI provider key, if you add one",
];

const clear = [
  { item: "Application status", why: "Needed to build your pipeline view and funnel stats." },
  { item: "Dates (applied, responded, etc.)", why: "Same reason — drives the timeline and reminders." },
  { item: "The numeric score", why: "Needed for sorting and filtering your pipeline." },
  { item: "ATS vendor and source portal", why: "Aggregate product analytics — which channels work — not about you specifically." },
  { item: "Plan tier", why: "Needed to enforce quotas." },
];
---

<Base
  title="Security"
  description="What's encrypted, what isn't, and why — the honest version of a security page. Key hierarchy, consented AI evaluation, and what happens if you lose your recovery key."
>
  <main class="page">
    <header class="page-header">
      <h1>What's actually encrypted</h1>
      <p>
        Most security pages promise more than the architecture actually delivers. This one
        doesn't. Here's the real split — and where it forced a genuine tradeoff.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h2>The honest split</h2>
      <div class="split">
        <div class="split-col split-col--encrypted">
          <h3>Encrypted client-side</h3>
          <p class="split-note">The server stores ciphertext it cannot read.</p>
          <ul>{encrypted.map((e) => <li>{e}</li>)}</ul>
        </div>
        <div class="split-col">
          <h3>Stored in clear</h3>
          <p class="split-note">Structural data the product needs to function.</p>
          <table>
            <tbody>
              {clear.map((c) => (
                <tr><td>{c.item}</td><td>{c.why}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p class="split-note">
            Company and role names get a <em>blind index</em> — a one-way hash we can match
            for filtering and duplicate detection, but never reverse back into the actual
            name. See "How search works without reading your data" below.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Why not encrypt everything?</h2>
      <p>
        Because a server that can't read your CV can't evaluate it against a job posting —
        that's not a limitation we're working around, it's math. Full end-to-end encryption
        and server-side AI evaluation are in direct tension: one requires the server to see
        plaintext, the other requires it never to. We picked a specific, narrow answer to
        that tension instead of pretending it doesn't exist.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How the key hierarchy works</h2>
      <ol class="hierarchy">
        <li>
          <strong>Your password</strong> never leaves your browser. It's run through
          Argon2id — a slow, memory-hard function specifically designed to resist
          brute-forcing — to derive your <strong>Master Key</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Your actual content is encrypted with a separate <strong>Data Encryption Key</strong>
          (DEK), which is itself encrypted ("wrapped") by your Master Key. This indirection
          means changing your password only re-wraps one small key, not every record you've
          ever saved.
        </li>
        <li>
          At signup, you get a <strong>recovery key</strong> — 24 ordinary words, shown once.
          It's a second way to unwrap your DEK if you forget your password. We do not store
          it, and we cannot resend it.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <div class="warning">
        <strong>If you lose both your password and your recovery key, your data is gone.</strong>
        Not "hard to recover" — genuinely unrecoverable, by us or anyone. That's the actual
        cost of a server that can't read your data. Write the recovery key down somewhere
        that isn't this browser.
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How an evaluation actually works</h2>
      <p>
        You click <em>Evaluate</em>. Your browser decrypts your CV and the job description
        and sends them, over TLS, for that one request only. The AI provider processes it in
        memory — never written to disk, never logged, the request body scrubbed from any
        error trace. The result comes back, your browser encrypts it, and that's what gets
        saved. Nothing server-side ever holds your plaintext CV at rest, and each evaluation
        is a deliberate action you take, not something that happens to your data in the
        background.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How search works without reading your data</h2>
      <p>
        Filtering your pipeline by company name needs to work, but the server still shouldn't
        know which companies you're targeting. The fix is a <em>blind index</em>: your
        browser derives a separate key from your DEK and uses it to compute a one-way hash of
        each company name. The server stores and matches on that hash — identical company
        names produce identical hashes, so filtering and duplicate detection work — but it
        can't run the hash backward to learn what the name was, and a different user's hash
        for the same company will never match yours.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What we log, and why</h2>
      <p>
        We track product behavior — feature usage, conversion, error rates, how long an
        evaluation takes — because that's how we know what to build next. We do not, and
        structurally cannot, track what's inside your CV or your reports. See our
        <a href="/legal/privacy">privacy policy</a> for the full list of what's collected.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="cta">
      <a href="/#waitlist">Get early access &rarr;</a>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .page { max-width: 46rem; margin: 0 auto; padding: var(--p-space-16) var(--p-space-6); }
  .page-header { margin-bottom: var(--p-space-12); }
  .page-header h1 { font-family: var(--p-font-display); font-size: var(--p-text-5xl); color: var(--p-porphyra-900); margin: 0 0 var(--p-space-3); }
  .page-header p { font-size: var(--p-text-lg); color: var(--color-muted); }
  section { margin-bottom: var(--p-space-12); }
  h2 { font-family: var(--p-font-display); font-size: var(--p-text-2xl); color: var(--p-porphyra-900); margin-bottom: var(--p-space-4); }
  h3 { font-size: var(--p-text-lg); margin: 0 0 var(--p-space-1); }
  p { color: var(--color-muted); }

  .split { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: var(--p-space-8); }
  .split-col { border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: var(--card-radius); padding: var(--p-space-6); }
  .split-col--encrypted { background: var(--color-success-soft); border-color: var(--p-verdigris-500); }
  .split-note { font-size: var(--p-text-sm); margin: 0 0 var(--p-space-4); }
  .split-col ul { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--p-space-2); }
  .split-col li { padding-left: var(--p-space-5); position: relative; color: var(--color-foreground); }
  .split-col li::before { content: "🔒"; position: absolute; left: 0; font-size: 0.75em; }
  .split-col table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: var(--p-text-sm); }
  .split-col td { padding: var(--p-space-2) 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); vertical-align: top; }
  .split-col td:first-child { font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-foreground); white-space: nowrap; padding-right: var(--p-space-3); }
  .split-col td:last-child { color: var(--color-muted); }

  .hierarchy { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--p-space-4); padding-left: var(--p-space-6); color: var(--color-muted); }
  .hierarchy strong { color: var(--color-foreground); }

  .warning {
    background: var(--color-warning-soft);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-warning);
    border-radius: var(--card-radius);
    padding: var(--p-space-4) var(--p-space-5);
    color: var(--color-warning-text);
    margin-top: var(--p-space-4);
  }

  .cta { text-align: center; }
  .cta a {
    display: inline-block;
    background: var(--button-primary-bg);
    color: var(--button-primary-fg);
    padding: var(--button-padding-y) var(--button-padding-x);
    border-radius: var(--button-radius);
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .split { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
</style>
